joining us now on the programme once  again the justice secretary robert  buckland mr buckland great to have you  on this morning  um and of course big a day for you big a  big set of announcements which which we  will deal with in depth but  but i do want to start with that uh  oprah interview and  and harry and megan's accusations of of  racism  and indeed of bullying i'm just  wondering did you have you seen the  interview  no i haven't i'm afraid uh like many  people i was uh  busy last night dealing with other  matters work chiefly so i've not seen it  sure i i do wonder though whether or not  you feel that  that such is the nature of the  allegations particularly when it comes  to  megan's mental health the accusation of  racism  whether you feel that you know the  palace almost needs to respond to that  well i don't think it's right to me as a  member of the government to start  commenting about matters that are  uh for the royal family i i think it's a  long established precedent  i i really uh i'm not able to comment uh  i don't know about the  detail of it i've not seen the interview  and i don't think it would be right to  me  to uh add my foreplay to to these  matters  i i i understand the point because it is  a carbon copy of the point that was made  by the prime minister at the downing  street news conference yesterday he said  that he has a rule that he doesn't talk  about the royal family i mean  it's obvious that that rule if it exists  gets broken quite a lot he often  invokes their service he often invokes  their their patriotism  you know even on the matter around  prince andrew he said that you know that  the royal family is  beyond reproach i mean on that i mean do  you think that the royal family is  beyond reproach is that why  it is you don't feel it's appropriate  for you to comment  well i support the work of the queen and  the royal family their devotion to  public duty is well known the hard work  that they put in  every year even during their covet  crisis is well chronicled  i think that the queen epitomizes public  duty and public service  and we all look up to her and admire her  for that as we do other members of the  royal family who work  hard on that element of public duty  is at the heart of what makes the  monarchy work here in the united kingdom  uh well let's turn our attention very  directly to your own set of  responsibilities your own duties a big  day  for the ministry of justice uh tell us a  bit about the plans that you're  announcing  well we've got a major new bill today  that is a joint bill with the home  office so it covers a wide range  of new measures most notably on  sentencing i will be introducing  important changes to the sentencing are  for example of child killers  who will now uh be able to receive whole  life  orders under a life sentence we're going  to change the way we sentence  um children particularly with regard to  the most serious offense  of murder but also we're doing a range  of changes uh on issues like causing  death by dangerous driving increasing  the maximum to life and a new uh  extension of the offense of sexual  activity uh in position in abusive a  position of trust  covering people like sports coaches and  faith leaders which up to now have not  been in the same category  as teachers and social workers for  example so a wide range  of important measures that i know will  help to make our streets safer and  increase public confidence indeed and on  the latter of those the the the loophole  around sports coaches faith leaders i  mean the nspcc very firmly in your  corner they're delighted by all of this  but i suppose that some people will ask  well look there are perhaps another  couple of categories that that might  have been included  music teachers driving instructors could  you just explain the rationale for not  including those in this  yes well what we're going to do without  being too technical is uh  legislate immediately on sports coaches  and music teachers we'll have a power in  the bill to include  in the future other categories but we  need to make sure that the evidence  base is very clear there's there's  obvious concern about some of the  activities in those  other areas you talk about but remember  we're dealing with young people of the  age of 16 and 17  in many other respects of course they're  they're they're allowed to  uh have sexual relations even to get  married  and therefore this is a very sensitive  area and we need to make sure that  whilst  uh you know the abuse of power which  this is often sadly about  needs to be rooted out we've got to  maintain a balance between the uh you  know the rights of young people  and also the need to protect them from  harm and the bill also granting  new powers to break up disruptive  protests i mean two words spring  instantly to mind extinction  rebellion i mean this is a direct  response to the climate movement isn't  it  well it's a response to the fact that  whilst freedom of expression and the  right to protest  are at the heart of our civil liberties  we've got to think about the  sometimes huge inconvenience caused to  other people going about their lawful  business on these particular provisions  dealing with  uh the need to notify the police as to  the the time  during which uh the protest will take  place other measures relating to  ease of access to parliament which is a  fundamental democratic right of itself  and also issues relating to noise i  think get the balance right between  freedom of expression  and of course the the rights of other  people as well  who choose not to take part in these  events but who  often face great inconvenience and  sometimes  worse as a result of what happens there  is i think even by your own admission  there's quite a lot in this bill that  deals with  longer sentences for for particular  crimes  our longer sentence is going to change  anything is this all just all about the  the punitive  aspect of the judicial process i mean  the prisoners reform trust  says there's not a shed of evidence to  show that this runaway inflation their  words and punishment reduces crime i  mean is there  evidence that we will see a reduction in  crime well  i think the most obvious evidence is  that that if you incarcerate people who  commit offences  that are a danger to the public and a  threat to the public you're reducing  crime you're protecting the public and i  think one of the most important  functions  of our prison system is indeed public  protection and we need to emphasize that  but at the same time there's a whole  raft of initiatives that we're taking  with regards to uh rehabilitation of  offenders  so in in this bill for example i'm going  to be taking powers to extend  uh the use of curfews uh which i think  is a very effective way of dealing with  people in the community  and there are lots of non-legislative  initiatives i'm taking on mental  health on addiction as well to deal with  some of the root causes of offending and  to rehabilitate those  who are capable of change the sad truth  is  that there is a group of people in our  society who are genuinely bad  and who we need protection from and this  is what this bill seeks to address  um minister let's talk about it  responsibilities um  covered and blocked over 19 in prisons  in particular  can you just give us the kind of latest  statistics the latest figures that  you've had in terms of  how many cases how many fatalities we've  experienced in the prison system  yes well over the last year we've lost  just over 80 prisoners as a result  directly off covid  a number of about 20 or 25 others uh  died within 28 days of a positive covid  test  that out of a population over the year  of  140 000 people have been through the  prison  system whilst losing anybody is a very  difficult  and sad moment the work that's been done  by prison staff  and the authorities to bear down on  covet has been  important and remarkable the way in  which it was done by quarantining new  entrants and by really ramping up the  testing program which has happened to  great success  has helped us bear down on this disease  we've had to take measures to restrict  a lot of the activities of prisoners  we've been able to do more in this  lockdown than we did before  and we're learning all the time but the  story in prisons has been one of control  and once it has been very challenging  for staff  sorry sorry to interrupt minister but  we're running out of time but just just  in terms of the  the push from jcvi to perhaps allow  prisoners to be vaccinated on a prison  by prison basis you know they go in  they vaccinate everyone they come out  rather than the way in which it's  working at the moment  following the kind of the most  vulnerable categories just just explain  that decision i mean  a number of prison officers including  one of my acquaintances i've pointed out  that you know  what the prison officers told lbc they  felt they felt like lambs to the  slaughter  you know my friend said something a bit  more sweary than that  well i think it's important that we  absolutely uh  drive on with the priorities that the  jcvi  has set and as other ministers have said  it's important that we  adopt their advice and uh carry it  through  and i think that in large measure we're  already working through  uh the business staff who have fall  within the existing categories the next  categories of course will follow and  the joint committee on vaccinations and  immunizations didn't they recommend that  local vaccinations  teams should be should be given the  flexibility to deal with prisons in a  single goal not work down the list  well well i think the the important  point here is that  prisoners will not get priority ahead of  any other part of the community a number  of prisoners have of course been  vaccinated  because they fall into the most  vulnerable category and i think  following  uh the community is a sensible way to go  of course we'll always  listen to and look at the evidence very  carefully uh and my particular  priorities to make sure the business  staff get inoculated as quickly  as possible  the jcvi did recommend that local  vaccination teams  should be allowed to go into individual  prisons vaccinate everyone i mean that's  the case isn't it they did recommend  that  well i think where we are is that  there's been an interim report  there will be a final decision made  later this  month i think it's right that we've set  out the broad contours here of the  vaccination  program which has been so successful  thus far  and i will repeat that my priorities to  make sure the prison staff are  vaccinated  and that visitors shouldn't get ahead of  the queue  uh but should be inoculated or  vaccinated in accordance with  uh the the priorities set for the rest  of the community  but just on a related topic and still  regarding covet i mean the prison's  watchdog says that  prisoners and have been locked in their  jails for for more than  nine tenths of the day to keep them safe  from covet 19. i understand that there's  not an awful lot of sympathy for people  who find themselves in prison  but that isn't acceptable is it well i  think like  the rest of the community prisoners have  found that  the restrictions have been greater on  them than would be normally the case  in this particular lockdown there has  been opportunity to do  more activities so essential work has  been carried out and there have been  more  activities and educational opportunities  and of course we've increa  increased video and telephone  communication with families  as well i think prisoners understand the  need for  those restrictions and have accepted  that uh it's in their own interests that  they are kept  safe and we've worked very hard with  them to make sure that even though  the restrictions might be greater there  have been things for them to do and  occupy themselves  with very tough calls were made early on  in this pandemic but the results have  been  uh important and i think they are a  testament to the achievement of staff  and indeed the prisoners themselves  in making sure that establishments once  we've seen outbreaks  have not resulted in the sort of  fatalities that could have been the case  had those corrective steps not been  taken

